        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Definitions of key terms",
      "pageStart": 10,
      "pageEnd": 11,
      "target": "../sections/sec-002-definitions-of-key-terms.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Age groups\nIn these guidelines the following definitions for adults, adolescents, children and infants are \nused in recommendations for specific age groups. It is acknowledged that countries may have \nother definitions under national laws.\n•\tAn adult is a person older than 19 years of age.\n•\tAn adolescent is a person 10–19 years of age inclusive.\n•\tA child is a person from one year of age to younger than 10 years of age.\n•\tAn infant is a child younger than one year of age.\nKey populations\nKey populations are defined as groups who, due to specific higher-risk behaviours, are at \nincreased risk",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Defines key terms used in the guidelines, including age groups for adults, adolescents, children and infants and WHO's definition of key populations. It also explains concepts such as substantial risk of HIV and notes that risk-informed PrEP can be cost-effective but that cost should not be the only consideration.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
