    {
      "id": "library/10665-360869/blocks/prose-sec-008",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "\u0003Background",
      "pageStart": 13,
      "pageEnd": 14,
      "target": "../sections/sec-008-11-background.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "HIV remains a major public health issue, with approximately 37.7 [30.2–45.1] million people \nliving with HIV and 1.5 [1.0–2.0] million new infections in 2020. Despite global commitments \nto reduce new infections by 75% between 2010 and 2020, to fewer than 500 000, new HIV \ninfections declined only by 31% over that decade (4). Key populations1 and their partners \naccounted for 65% of the new infections globally and for 93% of new infections outside sub-\nSaharan Africa. Adolescent girls and young women accounted for 25% of new infections in \nsub-Saharan Africa in 2020, despite constituting just",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the global HIV situation, with about 37.7 million people living with HIV and 1.5 million new infections in 2020, a decline well short of global targets, and high shares among key populations and young women in sub-Saharan Africa. It recalls the 2021 Political Declaration targets and WHO's existing PrEP recommendations. It explains the case for additional PrEP options and notes that risk-informed PrEP can be cost-effective while having value beyond cost.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-360869/blocks/prose-sec-009",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "\u0003Objective and intended audience",
      "pageStart": 14,
      "pageEnd": 15,
      "target": "../sections/sec-009-12-objective-and-intended-audience.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "This guideline is intended to provide an evidence-informed recommendation for CAB-LA as \nPrEP for HIV prevention to support countries in achieving national targets of reducing new HIV \ninfections. \nThe primary audience for this guideline is national and subnational programme managers \nand policy-makers responsible for the national health sector response to HIV, particularly in \nLMICs, and clinicians and other health care providers, particularly those working in primary \nhealth services, including lay and community health workers, who are the first point of contact \nfor people who could benefit",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Sets out the objective of providing an evidence-informed recommendation on cabotegravir as PrEP and names the intended audiences, from national programme managers and clinicians in low- and middle-income countries to people at risk, community organizations and donors.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-360869/blocks/prose-sec-010",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "\u0003Guiding principles",
      "pageStart": 15,
      "pageEnd": 16,
      "target": "../sections/sec-010-13-guiding-principles.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The following principles have informed the development of this guideline and should guide the \nimplementation of the recommendations:\n•\tThis guideline needs to be implemented within a public health and universal health coverage \nframework. In addition to strengthening HIV prevention services, the guideline should be \nimplemented with a view to strengthening broader health systems, especially primary and \ncommunity-based health services.\n•\tImplementation of the guideline needs to be accompanied by efforts to promote and protect \nequity and the human rights of people at substantial risk of HIV,",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Lists the guiding principles for implementing the cabotegravir guideline, including a public health and universal health coverage approach, equity and human rights, voluntary and stigma-free services. It also stresses adapting to local context and full participation of affected communities.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-360869/blocks/prose-sec-011",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "\u0003Methods for guideline development",
      "pageStart": 16,
      "pageEnd": 17,
      "target": "../sections/sec-011-2-methods-for-guideline-development.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "This guideline was developed in accordance with procedures established by the WHO \nGuidelines Review Committee (16). The recommendation in this guideline is based on the \nGrading of Recommendations Assessment, Development and Evaluation (GRADE) approach \nto reviewing evidence and formulating recommendations (17). Consistent with previous WHO \nguidelines, this guideline is based on a public health approach that considers effectiveness, \nacceptability, feasibility and resource needs across a variety of settings.\nAll external contributors to the development of the guidelines, including members of",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that the guideline followed WHO Guidelines Review Committee procedures and the GRADE approach, with declarations of interest from all external contributors. It lists the web annexes of evidence prepared and presented at meetings facilitated by an independent methodologist.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
